rand=[$1(i_brand)], i_brand_id=[$0(i_brand_id)], i_manufact_id=[$2(i_manufact_id)], i_manufact=[$3(i_manufact)], ext_price=[$4(ss_ext_sales_price)])\n?                    ++        ^            ++           ^                               ^                               ^\n\n+       LogicalAggregate(group=[{58, 59, 64, 65}], ext_price=[SUM($43)])\n          LogicalFilter(condition=[AND(=($0(d_date_sk), $28(ss_sold_date_sk)), =($30(ss_item_sk), $51(i_item_sk)), =($31(ss_customer_sk), $73(c_customer_sk)), =($77(c_current_addr_sk), $91(ca_address_sk)), =($35(ss_store_sk), $104(s_store_sk)), =(CAST($63(i_category)):CHAR(5), 'Books'), =($6(d_year), 2001), =($8(d_moy), 2), <>(SUBSTRING($100(ca_zip), 1, 5), SUBSTRING($129(s_zip), 1, 5)), =(CAST($99(ca_state)):CHAR(2), 'IL'), =($85(c_birth_month), 9), >=($39(ss_wholesale_cost), 73), <=($39(ss_wholesale_cost), 93))])\n            LogicalJoin(condition=[true], joinType=[inner])\n              LogicalJoin(condition=[true], joinType=[inner])\n                LogicalJoin(condition=[true], joinType=[inner])\n                  LogicalJoin(condition=[true], joinType=[inner])\n                    LogicalJoin(condition=[true], joinType=[inner])\n                      LogicalTableScan(table=[[date_dim]])\n                      LogicalTableScan(table=[[store_sales]])\n                    LogicalTableScan(table=[[item]])\n                  LogicalTableScan(table=[[customer]])\n                LogicalTableScan(table=[[customer_address]])\n              LogicalTableScan(table=[[store]])\n  \n```"}], 'response': 'Step 1: The logical plan changes after rewrite indicate that the query has been rewritten to apply aggregate functions and `GROUP BY` directly to the data source referred to in the `FROM` clause of the inner query before projection.\n\nStep 2: Parsing the logical plan changes, we can identify the following detailed changes:\n\n* The original query had a `LogicalProject` operator with multiple columns, including aggregates. After rewrite, this has been replaced by a single `LogicalProject` operator with only the necessary columns.\n* The aggregate functions have been applied directly to the data source referred to in the `FROM` clause of the inner query before projection.\n* The original query had multiple `LogicalJoin` operators and `LogicalTableScan` operators. After rewrite, these have been combined into a single `LogicalJoin` operator with multiple conditions.\n\nStep 3: Based on the query rewrite rule, we can identify that this rewrite matches Case 1 of the rule:\n\n* The SQL query contains a `GROUP BY` clause that groups by simple columns (no expressions in the `GROUP BY` list).\n* The aggregate functions in the `SELECT` clause operate directly on columns (no expressions inside the aggregate functions).\n* The `SELECT` clause only contains the columns used in the `GROUP BY` clause and the aggregates without any transformations or additional expressions.\n\nStep 4: According to the query rewrite rule, Case 1 applies when the conditions are met. In this case, the conditions are indeed met:\n\n* The original query has a `GROUP BY` clause that groups by simple columns (i.e., `i_brand`, `i_brand_id`, `i_manufact_id`, and `i_manufact`).\n* The aggregate functions in the `SELECT` clause operate directly on these columns (e.g., `sum(ss_ext_sales_price)`).\n* The `SELECT` clause only contains the columns used in the `GROUP BY` clause and the aggregates without any transformations or additional expressions.\n\nTherefore, the query rewrite rule applies Case 1 to the given SQL query.', 'time': 7.411664962768555}
